WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.405
Digests are binary values, but they are often encoded as hex or base64 for files, configuration, protocols, logs, databases, or user interfaces.

2
00:00:08.405 --> 00:00:11.080
Encoding changes representation, not underlying security strength.

3
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:20.249
A digest can be correct as bytes and still fail verification because one system expects uppercase hex, another expects lowercase hex, and a third

4
00:00:20.249 --> 00:00:21.778
includes whitespace or separators.

5
00:00:21.778 --> 00:00:23.688
Formats need to be explicit.

6
00:00:23.688 --> 00:00:27.509
Truncation must be deliberate and justified by the use case.

7
00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:31.711
Accidental truncation can remove security margin or create confusing compatibility behavior.

8
00:00:31.711 --> 00:00:38.206
Stored digest formats should include enough context to know the algorithm, output length, and any relevant parameters.

9
00:00:38.206 --> 00:00:44.319
This is especially important when systems may need to support older and newer formats during migration.

10
00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:49.668
A self-describing format also makes operational audits and future upgrades less error-prone.

11
00:00:49.668 --> 00:00:50.814
Comparison deserves attention.

12
00:00:50.814 --> 00:00:56.545
Validate length and encoding before comparing values, and fail safely when the format is invalid.

13
00:00:56.545 --> 00:01:04.187
For sensitive comparisons, use approved timing-safe comparison functions where appropriate instead of ordinary string comparisons that may exit early.

14
00:01:04.187 --> 00:01:09.917
Not every digest comparison has the same sensitivity, but review should make the choice intentional.

15
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:17.177
Digest handling is part of the security boundary because storage, parsing, and comparison determine what the program actually trusts.

16
00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:25.200
Clear errors for malformed digests are useful, but they should not reveal secret material or encourage fallback to a weaker format.
